  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between isocytisoside-7-O-glucoside and HepG2, a cell line commonly used for studying liver-related cancers. The evidence is derived from multiple pathways, with isocytisoside-7-O-glucoside being identified as a target in several biological processes. In one pathway, it is linked to SMMC-7721, which is targeted by Amarolide, and Amarolide is known to target HepG2. In another pathway, isocytisoside-7-O-glucoside is linked to SF-188, which in turn targets a complex compound that also has a target of HepG2. These findings suggest that isocytisoside-7-O-glucoside may act through a series of molecular interactions, ultimately influencing HepG2 through the pathways involving Amarolide and the complex compound. The evidence is consistent across different pathways, indicating a possible role in HepG2 biology, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The strength of the evidence is moderate, with some pathways showing slightly lower scores, but the overall pattern supports a potential relationship."
